      title: 'Magnetic Force and Work Done',
      explanation: 'The magnetic force on a charge $q$ moving with velocity $\\vec{v}$ in a magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ is given by $\\vec{F} = q(\\vec{v} \\times \\vec{B})$. Since the force is always perpendicular to the velocity ($\\vec{F} \\cdot \\vec{v} = 0$), the power delivered by the magnetic force is zero, meaning it does no work. Consequently, the speed and kinetic energy of the particle remain constant, while only the direction of motion changes.',
      example: 'A proton moving in a uniform magnetic field describes a circular path at constant speed; its kinetic energy remains unchanged, but its momentum vector is continuously rotating.',
      trap: 'Do not assume magnetic fields can speed up or slow down a free charged particle; they can only bend its path. Any change in kinetic energy requires an electric field.'
